+\begin{result}[The cubic moment in Tornheim--Witten derivatives]
+\label{integral:neg:cubic}
+A named depth-two evaluation is already known: see
+Bailey--Borwein--Borwein~\cite[Theorem~6]{integral:BBB2015}.
+Define
+\[
+ T(s,t,u)=\sum_{m,n\ge1}\frac{1}{m^s n^t(m+n)^u},
+ \qquad T_j=\partial_jT(1,1,1),\quad
+ T_{jk}=\partial_j\partial_kT(1,1,1),
+\]
+and put $a=\frac12\log(2\pi)$, $A=\gamma+\log(2\pi)$, and
+\[
+ V=\frac{\pi^2}{48}+\frac{A^2}{12}
+       -\frac{A\zeta'(2)}{\pi^2}+\frac{\zeta''(2)}{2\pi^2}.
+\]
+Then
+\[
+ \int_0^1\log^3\Gamma(x)\,dx
+ =a^3+3aV+\frac{3\zeta(3)}{16}
+ +\frac{3}{8\pi^2}
+     \left(2A^2\zeta(3)-2AT_3+2T_{13}-T_{12}\right).
+\]
+The derivatives are ordinary locally absolutely convergent derivatives
+at $(1,1,1)$. This is equivalent to the cited classical formula.
+A further reduction to a specified smaller basket of ordinary zeta
+values and derivatives is a separate research question. Failure of
+a finite PSLQ search in such a basket is not a nonmembership theorem.
+\end{result}
 
